## Database session authentication

Business resources must not use the reserved tables `auth_users`, `auth_sessions`, `auth_audit_logs`, or `auth_rate_limits`. The generator rejects conflicts before changing outputs, including during upgrades; it does not remove already deployed resource routes.

The console preserves the API's JSON number contract for full-range `int64`, defaults, and integers nested in JSON fields. This requires native BigInt, JSON source text access, and `JSON.rawJSON`; unsupported browsers reject large-number reads/writes rather than rounding them.

This project uses first-party credentials and revocable database sessions. `POST /auth/login` and `POST /auth/logout` are public (logout is idempotent); `GET /auth/me`, `POST /auth/password`, and every `/api/v1` route require a session. Session 401 responses intentionally omit `WWW-Authenticate`.

The migration source of truth is the GORM models `auth_users`, `auth_sessions`, and `auth_audit_logs`{{if .Options.IsPostgres}}, plus `auth_rate_limits` for shared login limiting{{end}}. No handwritten authentication-table migration is shipped. Before the first start, run `make migration name=session_auth`, review the SQL and commit it, then run `make migrate-apply`. Runtime startup never calls `AutoMigrate`. The fixed roles are `admin` (all generated permissions) and `viewer` (generated reads only); user role assignments are editable, but role definitions and permission sets are not.

SQLite unit tests create the same authentication models with test-only `AutoMigrate`, without Docker. AutoMigrate and Atlas independently render DDL: passing these unit tests does not prove the reviewed migrations apply or match the model schema. Keep the disposable-database Atlas apply/schema-diff gate as a separate check; PostgreSQL integration tests require an explicitly migrated disposable database.

On an empty `auth_users` table, startup fails unless both `AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL` and `AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` are set. Use a valid email and a unique bootstrap password of 12–1024 bytes; invalid credentials are rejected before creating any user. After the first successful start, remove both variables and immediately rotate the password through `/auth/password`; later starts ignore non-empty bootstrap values once any user exists.

Stored password encodings intentionally omit a leading `$`: `argon2id$v=19$m=<KiB>,t=<passes>,p=<lanes>$<salt>$<digest>` and legacy `pbkdf2-sha256$i=<iterations>$<salt>$<digest>`. Salt (16 bytes) and digest (32 bytes) use unpadded standard Base64. The Argon2id format is PHC-adjacent, not standard PHC; external PHC decoders are not directly compatible. Do not silently add the leading `$` or change the parser: any format change must preserve existing hashes through explicit compatibility/migration handling.

Passwords use Argon2id with the shipped OWASP configuration `m=19456,t=2,p=1`; PBKDF2-SHA256 stored hashes remain readable as the documented FIPS migration path. Tune Argon2id to roughly 100–300 ms on the deployment host. The active Argon2 working buffers use about `stored-hash memory × AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH_CONCURRENCY`: the default four slots mean 76 MiB for default hashes, or 256 MiB at the accepted stored-hash ceiling of 64 MiB. This is not a process RSS cap: GC-retained allocations, other application work and runtime overhead need additional headroom. KDF work is capped per process and rejects without queueing with 429 when full. Login/logout consume the IP budget; login also reserves an email attempt before KDF work (including unknown accounts and hash-capacity rejection). The email limit is three times `AUTH_LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT`, cleared on successful credential verification. Reserving before verification prevents concurrent failure overshoot. The per-IP key uses Echo `RealIP`. Set `HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS` to the comma-separated CIDRs of actual proxy hops (for example `127.0.0.1/32` for a host-local proxy); do not trust client networks or every address. Only these hops may supply `X-Forwarded-For`: private/link-local/loopback ranges are not implicitly trusted. Empty means direct-IP mode, with a production startup warning; behind a proxy this shares its budget. The proxy must overwrite/append the real client address correctly and the API must not be publicly reachable outside that topology. IPv4-mapped addresses share IPv4 quotas; IPv6 addresses share a /64 quota, which also groups legitimate clients on that subnet. Invalid addresses share one fallback bucket. Distributed sources can still exhaust capacity: retain edge rate controls and load-test the intended topology. Drain old replicas when changing IP-key semantics; mixed old/new binaries do not enforce a single shared quota.

Measure the actual password paths on the target host with `GOGC=100 GOMEMLIMIT=2GiB go test ./internal/platform/auth -run='^$' -bench='^BenchmarkPassword$' -benchmem -benchtime=1s -count=10 -cpu=1,4`. The benchmark includes Argon2id hash/verification and legacy PBKDF2-SHA256 verification at 600,000 iterations, each serially and with `RunParallel`. It uses synthetic credentials and no database. Record the hardware/toolchain, parameters and GC settings with the raw output; compare repeated samples using benchstat or equivalent statistics. Parallel `ns/op` is elapsed time divided by completed operations, not individual login latency; `B/op` is allocated bytes, not peak live memory/RSS. Run `-race` separately. These measurements exclude HTTP, database work, KDF admission/rejections and post-login rehash, and do not establish algorithm strength or a deployment SLO. The Go memory limit is a soft GC target, not a hard safety limit; choose CPU/concurrency and memory budgets appropriate to the host.

{{if .Options.IsPostgres}}PostgreSQL shares these budgets across processes and restarts, using database time and a short transaction that ends before hashing. All replicas must use the same database/schema and rate/window settings. Each keyspace is capped at 10,000 digests; full keyspaces reject new keys until expiry rather than evicting active budgets. Expired rows are removed on the next request. Database errors/timeouts fail closed with 500, not a process-local fallback. IP/email SHA-256 digests are not anonymization. A two-second deadline bounds each database operation; the table lock intentionally serializes low-volume authentication traffic, so load-test your deployment before scaling. Existing projects must explicitly migrate the new table before running the updated handlers; old binaries still use local limiting and must be drained before claiming a cluster-wide bound.

Disposable database check (deletes/fills `auth_rate_limits`; never use real data): `TEST_RATE_DATABASE_URL="..." TEST_RATE_DISPOSABLE=1 go test -race ./internal/platform/auth -run '^TestPostgresSharedLimiter$' -count=1`. It runs two separate processes and checks shared IP/email quotas, expiry, capacity, cancellation and fail-closed behavior. Run it separately from application tests.{{else}}SQLite uses bounded process-local LRU windows; run one application process. Multi-instance budgets require PostgreSQL, not a shared SQLite file.{{end}}

The cookie is `__Host-session` when `AUTH_SESSION_SECURE=true`, otherwise `session` for personal local development. Production rejects an insecure cookie configuration. Keep TLS and HSTS enabled in production because standard-library origin checking compares hosts without treating the URL scheme as a separate trust boundary.

The generated admin console is available at `/admin/`. Its resource registry, Zod schemas, form descriptors, and table columns are regenerated from `resources/*.yaml` and tracked in `.gobackend-generated.json`. Shared components and views are editable scaffold files that later resource generation leaves untouched. Admins can create, update, and delete records, while viewers receive a read-only UI backed by the same server authorization. Login/logout, password change, search, declared filters and sorts, pagination, responsive layouts, accessible dialogs, and light/dark/system themes are included.

Use `make frontend-install`, `make frontend-typecheck`, `make frontend-test`, and `make frontend-build`. `make frontend-dev` starts Vite with `/auth` and `/api` proxied to the Go server, preserving the session same-origin boundary. Production builds compile `web/dist` before the Go binary embeds it; Node.js and pnpm are not runtime dependencies.

`net/http.CrossOriginProtection` runs globally. Safe methods, `Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin` or `none`, requests carrying neither `Sec-Fetch-Site` nor `Origin`, and requests whose Origin host matches Host are allowed. The latter two are documented fail-open boundaries. Cross-origin CORS permission does not exempt a browser write. The generated Vite server proxies browser API traffic to the Go origin; no Vite origin is trusted in the production binary. No GET endpoint may change state.

429 rejections use the existing structured request log (`status=429`), not database audit INSERTs; production metrics count the 4xx class, not individual rejection reasons. Successful/failed authentication and business audits remain. Login finalization and password changes recheck the verified password hash inside their database transaction; a stale verification returns 401 and must be retried with current credentials.

Audit writes occur after a resource mutation commits and are best-effort. An audit failure is logged without changing the already-committed response, so a process crash in that small window can lose an event. Request bodies, passwords, tokens, and cookie values are never audited. `AUTH_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=0` keeps all events (default). A positive value explicitly enables hourly deletion of older events, in batches of 1,000 with a 100,000-row hourly cap. Archive required evidence before enabling retention.

Admins can use `/admin/users` to create accounts, assign the fixed admin/viewer roles, disable/re-enable accounts, and view/revoke active sessions. Users cannot change their own role/status; changes to other users revoke their sessions. The server rechecks the acting administrator inside the mutation transaction, including concurrent access changes. There is no user deletion or password-reset endpoint.

Admin-only APIs: `GET/POST /auth/users`, `PATCH /auth/users/:id`, `GET/DELETE /auth/users/:id/sessions`, `DELETE /auth/users/:id/sessions/:session_id`, and `GET /auth/audit-logs`. Lists accept `page` and `page_size` (1–100). User lookup accepts an exact `email`; audit lookup accepts exact `actor_id`, `action`, `resource`, `outcome`, plus RFC3339 `from` (inclusive) and `to` (exclusive). Audit rows are newest first and displayed at `/admin/audit`. Responses exclude password hashes and session token digests and use `Cache-Control: no-store`.

`pnpm frontend:e2e` runs real Chromium interactions using Playwright. It creates test accounts and changes passwords: use only a fresh, explicitly migrated disposable database, never real data. Build the frontend first and install Chromium with `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BROWSER_GC=1 pnpm exec playwright install chromium`, then run with `E2E_DISPOSABLE_DATABASE=1`. The test starts its own loopback server on port 4187 (`E2E_PORT` override), refuses to reuse an existing server, and stops it on exit. This HTTP harness targets the personal profile. The generator repository separately provides `scripts/frontend-e2e.sh production` with temporary SQLite and a test TLS proxy for Secure-cookie/cross-site checks; it does not install a production proxy or certificate in this project. Real domains, HSTS, trusted proxies and PostgreSQL deployment still require separate acceptance. Failure screenshots and traces go to gitignored `test-results/` and may contain test credentials.

Projects using `--auth session` require a generator version that understands that option; older generators fail closed when reading the project metadata.
{{end}}
